#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d2800e2-2d27-5622-a6a1-f5992cc92489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289579} {:answer/id #uuid "70d1fc46-bc8c-5ff3-988a-209dc0f6ad30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289580} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0a2aff-d31f-5dc6-9481-56985c8e3b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289581} {:answer/id #uuid "6202b5e7-d6f4-55ac-9284-153f48291dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289582} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0048b5-4130-5d93-bc47-76c1f1c47d63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6202b5e7-d6f4-55ac-9284-153f48291dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289582} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d7afee5-9159-52f5-88ce-0aa6c2474ac5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a63bb70d-731b-554c-adf7-37f9033998a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-103437" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/fb61c048eb7eac87f82c53717f8f67251b59610817ddf1f267e80543c0c86959.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=-1,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a critical point at $x=-1$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-1$ since $f(-1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63273267-c257-5c62-8e89-2b50792f1d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289586} {:answer/id #uuid "2042c9b3-c29e-5782-8171-8b3be12e32c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289587} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc3a8fc-b5aa-59b2-a411-b1f71fb7ac54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289588} {:answer/id #uuid "cd24d442-905c-56e7-b091-7fab665406c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289589} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbeab63-f015-57e1-85f1-08bd11c5c6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63273267-c257-5c62-8e89-2b50792f1d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289586} :answer-field/id #uuid "91463e95-1331-5fc1-a1c3-491eac483ed0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d69f67de-ee43-5100-8b00-e925c0e6171b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103438" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/5bbb0cacbe8dca2facfd49e757ba9b7aa40ee91ae820f001c72d637186d57b79.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=-1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement II is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=0,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=1$ since $f(1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6854c36a-035a-52b7-8473-c146e56038ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289593} {:answer/id #uuid "63f22c98-e5ea-5b29-b287-7fced162cf70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289594} {:answer/id #uuid "c34d4ce2-fd35-5755-ae97-2330aa24642e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289595} {:answer/id #uuid "a045385b-5e56-5adc-83f4-e625d79c4785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289596} {:answer/id #uuid "46e95f5f-eb72-5348-bd74-1fd5c7cb77d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the closed interval $[1,5]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e95f5f-eb72-5348-bd74-1fd5c7cb77d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the closed interval $[1,5]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289597} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0f31a4d-e408-58d2-9646-d443d0447989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6390010c-520d-5f4d-8e9b-7ad97c318ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47915" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/748abf694eac3c4e59fd09d3bc48648fe30e46561f536f9f9a16eac7eb526558.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the critical points?" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between  $x=1$ and $x=5$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ and\n\n- at $x=1$ and at $x=5$  there are sharp corners, hence ${f}^{′}(1)$ and ${f}^{′}(5)$ don't exist.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/48b786a6b9c270336ce507525acf2503439b2424dba59ed9ea200830a79dd828.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, all numbers in the closed interval $[1,5]$ are critical points for $f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf34da00-3c9c-5045-9857-9289dbf189d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289600} {:answer/id #uuid "df016bc0-b4ee-56d2-83d6-7e63864a8d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289601} {:answer/id #uuid "ee65c5e2-2e0c-5a42-9345-ef5a410d0027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289602} {:answer/id #uuid "53172910-3f6a-5916-bcfa-b58b71665829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289603} {:answer/id #uuid "9121759a-0d14-5a06-bde3-3aba59bb816e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df016bc0-b4ee-56d2-83d6-7e63864a8d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289601} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8c9a23e-330d-59db-ab11-90c929b1ea8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "126dc5db-7aa0-5e31-885e-35517971a655" :question/math-academy-id "q-47918" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/4a1843255ea718116bd2daa07f1375a3fb277c47ec8503fec90da824cd714d0b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,10],$ which points represent the global maximum of $f(x)$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest point is $D,$ and so this point represents the global maximum of $f(x).$ \n\n**Note:** The point $B$ is not a global maximum, it's a local maximum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218d63f5-bf0b-5234-a2f3-46f3cdf25280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289607} {:answer/id #uuid "b416071b-693d-54a7-8b6f-60c43474ddf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289608} {:answer/id #uuid "d544b0cd-6e69-5c1e-a53d-e4ec5f98fab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289609} {:answer/id #uuid "7724e61e-8754-5ff2-b2d2-63d8accf12c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289610} {:answer/id #uuid "af50d4ef-c9cb-5b02-acf4-28e9686f045c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7724e61e-8754-5ff2-b2d2-63d8accf12c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1201b52-31ab-51c5-82ce-d6f24e73cefb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c043bdf3-7728-5760-8821-193ed1e72bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-73401" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/48569328379c72215884b759687c7c40ced06f9864d05d2638e4389694f90630.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?" :question/worked-solution "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/576d25a420f1f4ebcbd0e9b055d7a27159caa8f1721ff5f35a32b86c3e3a0fb3.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is only one stationary point, and its $x$-coordinate is $x=0.$\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so ${f}^{′}(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point."}]}
